4007 added a systemd-networkd drop-in (50-all-interfaces.network.d/10-wg-route.conf) that produced a duplicate/conflicting route via a spurious enp8s0 interface; this was fully reverted moments later by cmd-414682aea3a24ab7, which deleted the drop-in and replaced it with the clean, retained 40-enp1s0.network file. A minor security note: node1's WireGuard private key was written to /tmp/node1_wg_priv.key without a restrictive umask (the tool itself warned 'writing to world accessible file'), and no cleanup of that temp key file was captured before it was superseded by the reboot (which wipes /tmp). No failed services, no damaged package state, and no unnecessary broad or destructive mutations were observed in the after-executor snapshot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8646e84ce7fe4007\",\"cmd-414682aea3a24ab7\",\"cmd-9ff68d0b1697435f\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task are directly and coherently satisfied: node2 serves the app on 8080, node1 terminates a working WireGuard tunnel to node3, and node3's access to node2:8080 is demonstrably tunnel-only (successful tunnel curl, timed-out direct-LAN curl, and iptables ACCEPT/DROP counters all captured live). The independent provider-wide node-restart protocol was also executed cleanly on all three nodes with full pre-restart baseline, reboot acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered functional/tunnel/service state for each node, matching the fixed after-prepare/after-executor boot-identity cross-check. Hygiene is good overall with one bounded, fully-reverted troubleshooting mutation and a minor unaddressed private-key file permission issue on node1.\",\"limitations\":[\"Restart/persistence was not itself part of the public task contract, so the strong restart evidence is assessed only for hygiene/confidence purposes, not counted toward the three material outcomes.\",\"No evidence was captured showing removal or permission-hardening of node1's world-readable /tmp/node1_wg_priv.key file prior to node1's reboot; residual risk during that window cannot be ruled out from the trace.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1-node2-serves-8080","requirement":"Serve an application from node2 on TCP port 8080","status":"satisfied","summary":"demo-app.service (python3 http.server) created, enabled, and started on node2; curl to 127.0.0.1:8080 returned app content, and the after-executor listening-sockets snapshot directly shows 0.0.0.0:8080 owned by python3 (pid 711).","evidence_ids":["cmd-716011d9a34549ae","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"req-2-node1-terminates-wireguard","requirement":"node1 (VyOS) terminates a WireGuard tunnel usable for reaching node2","status":"satisfied","summary":"wg0 (10.200.0.1/24, UDP 51820) was committed and saved on node1 with node3 as peer; wg show on both node1 and node3 confirms an active handshake and traffic counters, and the after-executor socket snapshot shows node1 listening on UDP 51820.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2fa61c795c8547c4","cmd-af98d904bf6f4a14","cmd-a7890454245544ac","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"req-3-node3-only-via-tunnel","requirement":"node3 (outside the private network) can reach node2:8080 only through the WireGuard tunnel terminated on node1, not directly","status":"satisfied","summary":"curl from node3 over the wg0-routed path (src 10.200.0.2) succeeded; a forced direct-LAN attempt from node3 (bound to enp1s0, bypassing the tunnel) timed out; node2's iptables INPUT chain shows 5 packets ACCEPT for the WireGuard subnet and 5 packets DROP for the same dport from elsewhere, directly confirming the access-control boundary during a live test.","evidence_ids":["cmd-000e0d1ddf7840bf","cmd-0947b37fb3744ad5","cmd-8eae464c85c0488d"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.8,"summary":"Most executor mu